<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.2.1: Squares in a Geometric Pattern</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01750</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.2.1: Squares in a Geometric Pattern</md:title>
    <md:uuid>10e75c2b-09e4-4fac-a690-7683e098e817</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Warm Up (5 minutes)</title>
<para>This warm up encourages students to quantify the number of squares in a geometric pattern by looking for and making use of structure. The thinking involved here prepares students to reason about other patterns that illustrate quadratic relationships.</para>
<title>Launch</title>
<para>Arrange students in groups of two. Give students 2 – 3 minutes of quiet time to think individually, and then ask them to share their thinking with their partner.</para>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>How does each expression represent the number of small squares in the figure?</para>
<para><figure><media alt="An image of a block of squares. There are eight squares on rows 1, 2, 3 and 4 and five squares on rows 5 - 6. There are six squares missing from the upper right corner of the block."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/5b2f937385dd355382040846e4eb54b535d7ba6b"/></media></figure></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Expression A: \(6 \cdot 8−2 \cdot 3\)
    
    <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> There are 6 rows with 8 small squares, but from this, 2 rows of 3 small squares have been removed.
  </item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Expression B: \(4 \cdot 8+2 \cdot 5\)
    
    <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> There are 4 rows of 8 small squares, and to this, 2 rows of 5 small squares are added.
  </item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>Expression C: \(8+8+8+8+5+5\)
    
    <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> This counts each row, starting from the bottom.
  </item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>Expression D: \(5 \cdot 6+3 \cdot 4\)
    
    <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> There are 5 columns of 6 small squares and 3 columns of 4 small squares.
  </item>
</list>
<title>Activity Synthesis</title>
<para>Make sure students see how each expression tells us about a way the squares can be counted or how the area of the figure can be determined. If time permits, ask students if they could generate a different expression that also represents the number of squares in the figure.</para></section>
</content>
